<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mo Li Hua</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">usually dated to the Qianlong reign (<a href="/years/1735">1735</a>–<a href="/years/1796">1796</a>); earliest lyrics in the Ming Wanli period; transcribed <a href="/years/1793">1793</a>, printed in Europe <a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as 茉莉花, Mòlìhuā</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Mo Li Hua&quot;, &quot;Jasmine Flower&quot;, is a Chinese folk song of the xiaodiao or &quot;short tune&quot; genre, popular in the towns of the Jiangnan region south of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>.</p></div>
